(project pricklypear-philosophy
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: philosophy")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section philosophy
  (doc "Design principles for the single-image habitat")
  (status implemented)
  (* agent note (|
     BOOK INTRO SLOT — philosophy — why this system exists
     Write your author voice in a human comment immediately below this note:
       (* roerick (|
          …
       |) *)
     Editorial guidance (agent): Open the book. Not a feature list — a stance. Why a live image + Postgres + Lisp, and what you refuse (e.g. dual product skins, brochure-as-truth). Keep it short enough to read on paper before principle 1.
  |) *)

  (verify
   (build "dune build @all")
   (smoke "one image process + Postgres object store + htmx UI"))
  (details
   (doc (|
        These principles describe the system as it runs today:
        one unix image binary, Postgres as durable store, Lisp
        product shell, multi-user via sessions and branches.

        Historical multi-process / Mirage-fleet language lived
        here in early drafts; that topology is retired. Solo5
        and process isolation remain optional later hardening
        (see spikes/), not v1 product claims.

        1. One live image. The running OCaml process is the OS
           surface: HTTP, REPL, eval, auth. No supervisor fleet
           or authd sidecar in the hot path. Rebuild from git +
           Postgres rows; do not treat the process as the disk.

        2. Postgres is the filesystem. All durable state is
           rows (function_defs, type_defs, view_bindings,
           data_rows, auth, AI sessions, …). The image is
           ephemeral and rebuildable from the store + package
           seed.

        3. Lisp-1, homoiconic. The AST is the source. Functions
           persist as JSONB. Editing the AST is editing the
           code. Closures, let, lambda, application are first-
           class; macros can come later.

        4. Two-tier change model. OCaml (primitives, HTTP, store,
           auth gate) changes slowly via rebuild/deploy. Lisp
           (user functions AND product environment UI — nav,
           home, routes, theme) changes fast via row updates.
           OCaml must not ship a silent second product skin;
           host/gate/emergency only (image.system-browser).

        5. Branches, not namespaces. system/main is the package
           baseline; each user has username/personal as an
           inheriting garden. Code branches; shared data. Fork
           is cheap; merge/promote is the careful operation.

        6. Multi-user by session and branch. Isolation is not
           per-process unikernels in v1. Many humans share one
           image; identity + branch scope their code world.
           Stronger process isolation is a later product choice.

        7. Author-runs-as-author. A function executes with its
           author's data permissions where that model applies —
           honest setuid-shaped provenance, not caller-spoofing.

        8. SERIALIZABLE by default. Mutating store paths default
           to SERIALIZABLE transactions. Weaker isolation is
           opt-in; most Lisp never sees the dial.

        9. Additive-only schema evolution. Types and migrations
           grow; they do not casually drop or rename. Cruft may
           accumulate; correctness and simple deploys matter more.

        10. Inspectability is non-negotiable. Functions, types,
            branches, routes, rows, and sessions are queryable
            and renderable in the htmx UI (and via /api/eval).
            The store must stay the most inspectable layer.
   |))
  )
 )
)
